                    <msContents><!-- describe the intellectual content of an inscription -->
                        <summary>This inscription is engraved onto two sides of a stone set up in front of the Jaina temple at Kolanupaka. According to the editors, the numerical date could be either Śaka 909 or 969. The Kalyāṇa Cālukya king mentioned is Āhavamalla, 
                            an epithet used for both Taila II (r. 973-996) and Sōmēśvara I (r. 1044-1068), meaning that this inscription could be dated to either reign. It records the grant made by several bodies including the <foreign>aṟuvaru</foreign>, <foreign>seṭṭiyaru</foreign> (merchants), “the five hundred” and “the twenty.”
                        </summary>
                        <!-- offers the possibility to give a summary of the inscription's content -->
                    </msContents>
